                "reviews": [
                    {
                        "recommendationid": "238045103",
                        "author": {
                            "steamid": "76561199211854452",
                            "num_reviews": 10,
                            "playtime_forever": 16647,
                            "playtime_last_two_weeks": 2881,
                            "playtime_at_review": 9549,
                            "last_played": 1794183317
                        },
                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "DMC 1: This game still rocks. Sure, the controls and the amount of moves you can do are much more simple than the later games. But the game is still extremely fun to play, the combat feels good, enemies are engaging to fight, the music and more horror-oriented atmosphere are peak, and the story does exactly what it needs to to set up the rest of the franchise despite being really silly at times. \n\nDMC 3: Still a masterpiece. This game fully understood the assignment on how to build upon everything the first game established. From the multi-style combat system and expanded character moves and combos that cemented the \"character action\" element of the franchise that stuck around for the next two games, as well as the story that serves as a prequel to the first game which is not only amazing itself with the crazy high octane action you would look for in this kind of game, but it also heavily deepens and contextualizes the world and the characters of the first game and makes the emotional impact hit even harder. The boss roster is also great (most of the time... looking at you mission 19) with possibly one of the best final bosses in any game.\n\nDMC 2: ...............\n\nOverall, 10/10. One of the best two-game collections you will find out there. Still wonder why Capcom never made a DMC 2.",
